# Domain Skin: {DOMAIN_NAME}

> Type: code | article | book | config | plan | research
> Modality: text | creative | research
> Applies to projects where `config.md` contains `domain.type: {DOMAIN_NAME}`

---

## Project Config Schema (relevant fields)

The domain skin is activated per-project by the following `domain:` block in `projects/{PROJECT}/config.md`:

```yaml
domain:
  type: code | article | book | config | plan | research | <custom>
  skin: templates/domain_skin.{type}.md   # or a project-specific path
  artifact:
    primary: <artifact noun>
    secondary: [<artifact noun>, ...]
  verify:
    method: build | cite_check | readability | parse | dry_run | recompute | doc_alignment | flux_check | <custom_method_name>
    command: "<shell command or N/A>"
    success_criteria: "<prose assertion>"
    # Optional: define a custom verify.method not in the 8 canonical
    custom_methods:
      <custom_method_name>:
        command: "<shell or tool invocation>"
        success_criteria: "<what PASS looks like>"
        failure_modes: ["<mode 1>", "<mode 2>"]
        independence_check: "<what 'independent verification' means>"
  metrics:
    - name: B_modality
      value: text | creative | research
    - name: strictness
      value: strict | relaxed
  ood_scope: text-discrete | structured-continuous | continuous
  languages: [en] | [en, ru] | [en, fr, de] | ...    # default [en] if omitted
  # RIGOR_LEVEL axis (v10 Bundle D) — see eradev.md §III.3
  rigor_default: strict | structured | exploratory | emergent | mixed
  rigor_map:
    - segment: "(name or glob of artifact segment — e.g., 'methods', 'src/api/**', 'chapter_body')"
      level: strict | structured | exploratory | emergent
      rationale: "(why this level for this segment — required for audit)"
  examples: [...]
  subdomains: [...]
```

### `rigor_default` + `rigor_map` schema (v10+)

- **Purpose:** declare the RIGOR_LEVEL baseline for this project (`rigor_default`) and per-segment overrides (`rigor_map`). See `eradev.md` §III.3 for the axis and §VI.1 FLUX protocol for exploratory/emergent segment behavior.
- **`rigor_default` values and meanings:**
  - `strict` — fully-specified spec; convergent output; full BIM (μ-check BLOCKING, Unified Plan mandatory, F7 hard threshold). Default for `code` and `config`.
  - `structured` — mostly-specified; mostly-convergent; partial BIM (Unified Plan required; μ-check BLOCKING with N/A-tolerance; F7 as COMMIT target). Default for `plan` and `article`.
  - `exploratory` — directional goal; divergent-with-target; BIM OFF → FLUX protocol (μ-check ADVISORY, Voice Guide substitutes Unified Plan, Validator advisory, long cycles permitted). Default for `book`.
  - `emergent` — spec emerges from work; fully divergent; no planning gate; post-hoc synthesis only.
  - `mixed` — project-level declaration that per-segment `rigor_map` is REQUIRED (no single default applies). Used for `research` (methods strict, exposition structured, hypothesis-formation exploratory) and article projects whose sections span levels.
- **`rigor_map` per-segment overrides:** optional list of `{segment, level, rationale}` entries. `segment` may be a section name ("methods", "introduction"), a file glob ("src/experimental/**"), or a named chapter/module. Analyst at RT-1 fills in per-segment overrides when a task genuinely spans multiple RIGOR_LEVELs. Absence = `rigor_default` applies to all segments.
- **Per-domain recommended defaults** (starting point; projects override as needed):

  | Domain | rigor_default | typical exploratory/emergent segments |
  |---|---|---|
  | code | strict | `experimental/**`, `scratch/**`, `prototypes/**` |
  | config | strict | (rare — most config is strict) |
  | plan | structured | `ideation/**`, discovery phases |
  | research | mixed | methods → strict; results → structured; discussion/intro → exploratory; hypothesis formation → emergent |
  | article | structured | introduction → exploratory; discussion → exploratory; methods → strict; abstract → structured |
  | book | exploratory | cold_open → emergent; reference_table → strict; formulas_block → strict; chapter_body → exploratory |

- **Backward compatibility:** projects WITHOUT `rigor_default` or `rigor_map` inherit the domain-derived default above. Existing `code` projects keep their current behavior (strict); book projects get a sensible creativity-preserving default (exploratory → FLUX for chapter bodies). Articles default to `structured` which is a minor relaxation vs full strict BIM; for articles with pre-existing strict discipline, set `rigor_default: strict` explicitly in project config.
- **RT protocol consequence:** `rigor_map.effective` (the resolution of domain → task → segment cascade) gates μ-check behavior at `meta.md` §μ-check modality branch, gates Unified Plan vs Voice Guide selection, and determines whether Invariant #17 τ_cycle primacy applies.

### `domain.languages` field (v10+)

- **Purpose:** declare the human-language set the project's bilingual / multilingual sync rule applies to. Replaces hardcoded RU+EN assumption from the legacy bilingual invariant (BL-19 pre-v10).
- **Default:** `[en]` — single-language, English only. Projects without this field are treated as English-only.
- **Common values:** `[en]`, `[en, ru]`, `[en, fr]`, `[en, zh]`, etc.
- **Interpretation:** files ending in `_<lang>.md` (or equivalent convention declared per-project) are considered translations of each other. Any edit to one MUST be mirrored to the others in the same commit — but ONLY when `languages.length > 1`.
- **Single-language projects** (`languages: [en]` or field omitted): bilingual-sync gates do NOT fire. The project has no mirror to maintain.
- **Multi-language projects** (`languages.length > 1`): bilingual/multilingual sync rule is active. Coherent enforces it at RT-2.

### `verify.method` is extensible (v10+)

- **Canonical methods (ship with framework):** `build`, `cite_check`, `readability`, `parse`, `dry_run`, `recompute`, `doc_alignment`, `flux_check` — the first 6 cover the default set of v10 domains (code, article, book, config, plan, research); the 7th, `doc_alignment`, is the v10 plan-class verify method for strict/structured RIGOR_LEVEL (runs at μ-check, between RT-1 close and Builder spawn — see entry below); the 8th, `flux_check`, is the v10 Bundle D verify method for exploratory/emergent RIGOR_LEVEL work (advisory, NOT blocking — see entry below).
- **Custom methods (per-project):** projects with specialized verification needs MAY define their own method by:
  1. Setting `verify.method: <custom_method_name>` in the project's `domain:` block.
  2. Adding a `verify.custom_methods.<custom_method_name>:` sub-block with `command`, `success_criteria`, `failure_modes`, and `independence_check`.
- **Examples of custom methods** (not shipped, projects can define):
  - `statute_check` — legal drafts validated against a jurisdiction's current statute set.
  - `contraindication_scan` — medical protocol drafts scanned against a drug-interaction / allergen database.
  - `ingredient_check` — culinary recipes validated against allergen / substitution lists.
  - `accessibility_audit` — UI configs validated against a11y rules (WCAG, ARIA).
- **Registry semantics:** `verify.method` is a REGISTRY. The 7 canonical methods are the framework's default set; projects extend with custom methods as their domain demands. The skin vocabulary map (`test / tests pass`, `validate`, `verify`) is bound to whichever method the project declares — canonical or custom. Coherent, Validator, and Builder agents read `verify.method` from the project config and act accordingly; they do NOT hard-code the canonical methods.

#### Canonical method: `doc_alignment` (v10+, plan-class artifacts)

- **Definition:** verifies that documentation layers are mutually consistent BEFORE the code / artifact-production phase. Distinct from `build` (code passes), `cite_check` (citations resolve), etc. — `doc_alignment` tests the PLAN as a document against a documented quality bar.
- **Triggers:** between RT-1 close and Builder spawn (the μ-check gate, see `meta.md` §μ-check). Coherent-owned.
- **Checks (all required):**
  1. `templates/unified_plan.md.template` has been instantiated as `reports/unified-plan-{TASK_ID}-{DATE}.md` and every section is populated (or marked N/A with a one-line justification).
  2. Cross-Layer Contracts table populated for multi-layer tasks (≥2 architectural layers).
  3. User Flow section populated for UI / consumer-facing tasks.
  4. Every AC maps to ≥1 artifact in Agent Assignments; every FM has a mitigation in the plan.
  5. No dangling references between sections (every mentioned artifact / BL / lesson resolves to an existing path or known ID).
  6. AC / FM present and count within spec (AC 3-8, FM 2-5).
  7. F7 sufficiency threshold documented per-layer: `B_received ≥ B_min` (not equality). B_min per layer currently set by operator judgment; v11 target = empirical calibration from task corpus.
- **Success criteria:** all 7 checks PASS → emit `[μ-check OK]` → Builder cleared. Any FAIL → block Builder spawn, return to Analyst / Visionary for missing sections.
- **Failure modes:** "plan reads polished but misses layer" (catches via check 2); "AC without artifact" (check 4); "FM without mitigation" (check 4); "dangling reference to a deleted BL / lesson" (check 5); "over-polished plan that starves Andon" (F10 anti-pattern — plans MUST be minimum-sufficient, not maximum-fidelity).
- **Independence check:** Coherent runs `doc_alignment` on its OWN synthesized plan — so the "independence" here is temporal (post-synthesis audit), not agent-role. For XL tasks a L2 Coherent MAY audit an L1 Coherent's output for additional independence.
- **Calibration (v10):** B_min thresholds per layer are currently static (operator judgment). v11 target = empirical calibration from accumulated corpus of `reports/unified-plan-*.md` artifacts with pass/fail history.
- **RIGOR_LEVEL applicability (v10 Bundle D):** `doc_alignment` applies ONLY to RIGOR_LEVEL ∈ {strict, structured}. For exploratory/emergent, use `flux_check` (below) instead. The μ-check modality branch (see `meta.md` §μ-check) selects which verify method runs per segment.
- **Rationale:** plan admissibility (old RT-1 implicit gate) ≠ plan completeness. `doc_alignment` makes the completeness check explicit and auditable.

#### Canonical method: `flux_check` (v10+ Bundle D, exploratory/emergent artifacts)

- **Definition:** advisory verification for RIGOR_LEVEL ∈ {exploratory, emergent} segments. Distinct from `doc_alignment` (BLOCKING gate for strict/structured) — `flux_check` is ADVISORY and does NOT block commit. See `eradev.md` §VI.1 FLUX RT Protocol.
- **Triggers:** at the μ-check position between RT-1 close and Builder spawn, for any segment where `rigor_map.effective ∈ {exploratory, emergent}`. Coherent-owned.
- **Checks (all advisory):**
  1. Voice Guide (or Unified Plan in degraded form) exists at `reports/voice-guide-{TASK_ID}-{DATE}.md` or `reports/unified-plan-{TASK_ID}-{DATE}.md`.
  2. IFR is present (may be directional / experiential rather than past-tense measurable).
  3. Ô declared for the voice-keeper agent (per Habit #2, universally applied).
  4. Draft exists (or draft plan exists) AND is "good-enough-to-iterate" — the next iteration or reader can engage with it.
  5. Voice is coherent across prior segments (if cross-segment work); legitimate divergences documented with rationale (voice intentionally shifts, new theme introduced).
  6. B_received ≥ B_min where B_min is the target-for-this-work (not minimum-permissible-any-task); if target is aesthetic (voice/rhythm), B_min is a soft guidance, not a hard threshold.
  7. Exit criterion declared: "the next pass can start from this" — AND the segment has not degenerated into circling (≥ N iterations without direction change triggers transition-to-structured proposal per §VI.1 mode transitions).
- **Success criteria:** all 7 checks PASS → emit `[μ-check ADVISORY: all green — FLUX segment continues]` → Builder cleared. Partial FAIL → emit `[μ-check ADVISORY: {findings}]` → Builder still cleared (operator / Visionary may choose to act on findings or transition to structured).
- **Failure modes:** "voice drift across chapters" (catches via check 5); "iteration count exceeds budget without direction change" (check 7 → transition proposal); "B_received below aesthetic target" (check 6 → advisory, not blocking); "Ô not declared" (check 3 → Habit #2 violation, still universal).
- **Independence check:** Coherent runs `flux_check` on its own synthesized Voice Guide — temporal independence (post-synthesis audit). Unlike `doc_alignment`, `flux_check` does not need a blocking gate because advisory findings are acted on by the operator / Visionary rather than mechanically enforced.
- **Rationale:** exploratory/emergent work cannot be accurately gated by docs-alignment checks because the spec itself emerges during the work. `flux_check` provides a light audit (did the segment produce something good-enough-to-iterate? is voice coherent? are iterations making progress?) without the over-specification penalty that `doc_alignment` would impose on creative/directional work.

#### Inter-rater reliability for subjective verify methods (v10+ Galileo Cronbach α, NEXT-C1)

The four canonical subjective verify methods — `readability`, `dry_run`, `flux_check`, `doc_alignment` — produce judgments rather than mechanical pass/fail signals (unlike `build` / `parse` / `recompute` which compile, parse, or numerically recompute). Subjective verifiers MUST sample N≥3 independent raters (LLM judges or human reviewers) and compute Cronbach α as the inter-rater reliability metric for the rating set. α below the per-method threshold = re-Validator round (re-sample with fresh raters, fresh seeds, or refined rubric) BEFORE the verdict is accepted. This operationalizes the Galileo 2026 LLM-as-judge calibration pattern (see `reports/rt5_research_new_approaches_2026-04-25.md` row 15) for EraDev's subjective verifiers and gives Validator's E_A a defensible reliability floor instead of single-rater self-report.

| Subjective method | Cronbach α threshold | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| `readability` | ≥ 0.70 | Standard psychometric bar; readability scores are well-studied and raters should converge cleanly. |
| `dry_run` | ≥ 0.65 | Plan dry-runs involve more interpretive judgment about realism / completeness; modest relaxation. |
| `flux_check` | ≥ 0.60 | Lower bar — exploratory/emergent segments deliberately permit divergent voice; full convergence not the goal, but raters must still substantially agree the draft is good-enough-to-iterate. |
| `doc_alignment` | ≥ 0.70 | Plan-completeness checks (AC↔artifact mapping, FM mitigation presence) are largely deterministic; raters should converge tightly. |

Operationally: rater set composition (mix of LLM judges, human reviewers, or both) is project-defined; α computation runs operator-side (NOT a framework hook in this v10 increment — runtime tooling deferred to v11). The Validator role MUST log {N_raters, per-rater scores, α, threshold, pass/fail} alongside its verdict in `reports/`. α below threshold ≠ "the artifact is bad" — it means "the verdict is not yet reliable, re-sample." This preserves Invariant #11 (Builder→Validator sequencing) while adding a reliability gate on the Validator output itself.

